Fix PluginCLAP::stateLoad() losing parameters on chunked clap_istream reads

Generated against DPF commit 4238e1c7f0351bbe488d79f0899c540543ac7583
("Merge branch 'develop'", the commit pinned by AUDIOPLUGINS_DPF_GIT_TAG in
Hex/CMakeLists.txt). File: distrho/src/DistrhoPluginCLAP.cpp.

WHAT IS BROKEN
--------------
DPF serializes the default (parameter-only) plugin state as a flat stream of
NUL-separated fields wrapped in sentinel tokens:

    "__dpf_parameters_begin__\0" "hex\0" "25\0" "__dpf_parameters_end__\0" "\xfe\0"

stateLoad() scans that stream with a 512-byte buffer. Because a field can span
several stream->read() calls, it bounds each strlen() by temporarily writing a
synthetic NUL at buffer[read] (the first byte past the current chunk), then
restoring the original byte. It then decides "this field is complete" with:

    if (buffer[i] == '\0')

That test cannot distinguish the two cases it has to distinguish:

  a) strlen() stopped at a REAL embedded NUL inside the chunk -> field complete;
  b) strlen() ran to the end of the chunk and stopped on the SYNTHETIC NUL
     -> the field is only partially read, the rest arrives in the next chunk.

In case (b) buffer[read] has just been restored to whatever byte was there
before, which for a chunked read is uninitialized/stale stack memory, so the
branch is taken or not depending on garbage. When it is wrongly taken, the
parser declares a truncated fragment (e.g. "__dpf_p") to be a complete field:
the "__dpf_parameters_begin__" sentinel comparison never matches, queryingType
never advances from 'i' to 'p', and Plugin::setParameterValue() is therefore
never called for ANY parameter for the remainder of that stateLoad(). The
parameter silently keeps its pre-load value.

CLAP's clap_istream_t::read() contract (distrho/src/clap/stream.h) explicitly
allows a short read -- `size` is a ceiling, not a guarantee -- and
clap-validator's `state-reproducibility-buffered` test deliberately feeds the
plugin a small prime number of bytes per call, which is exactly how this is
triggered. Any DPF plugin whose serialized state contains a sentinel token that
a chunked host reader can split across a read() boundary is affected; this is
not specific to any one plugin.

THE FIX
-------
Replace the ambiguous `buffer[i] == '\0'` completion test with `i < read`.
After `i += strlen(buffer + i)`, `i` can only be strictly less than `read` if
strlen() stopped at a real embedded NUL before reaching the synthetic
terminator position; `i == read` means the chunk ran out mid-field. Partial
accumulation into key/value already happens correctly before this test, and
key/value/fillingKey/queryingType live outside the outer read loop, so the
fragment simply continues accumulating on the next chunk.

This is behaviour-preserving for unchunked hosts: when read == sizeof(buffer)-1
the pre-loop `buffer[sizeof(buffer)-1] = '\xff'` sentinel already made the old
test evaluate false at i == read, i.e. the same answer `i < read` gives, and at
a genuine terminator both tests are true.

VERIFICATION
------------
A byte-faithful standalone C++ re-implementation of this scan loop, fed DPF's
real stateSave() wire format and a mock chunked clap_istream_t, was run over
chunk sizes 1..600 x 4 different stack-fill patterns, for both a small (57-byte,
single-read) and a large (2495-byte, multi-full-511-byte-read) state:

    unpatched: 28/2400 (small state) and 510/2400 (large state) scenarios lose
               parameter values
    patched:   0/2400 in both

End to end, clap-validator 0.4.1 against Hex.clap goes from
"30 passed, 1 failed, 2 warnings, 11 skipped" to
"31 passed, 0 failed, 2 warnings, 11 skipped".

NOTE: distrho/src/DistrhoPluginVST3.cpp has the same construct at the
equivalent line (~1005). It is deliberately NOT patched here: pluginval does not
currently exercise a chunked VST3 IBStream read, so the bug is unproven there
and out of scope for this change.

Full root-cause investigation: task-4-report.md / task-5-report.md under
Common/.superpowers/sdd/lets-breakdown-next-unfinished-breezy-shell/
(session-local; the summary above is self-contained on purpose).

Not reported upstream yet -- that is a separate, deliberate decision.

